    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm having some trouble with a series of walnut cabinet builds. I'm cutting dovetail joints, but I'm getting consistent splintering at the very tips of the pins. It's messing up the fit and making the joinery look messy. Any advice on how to manage the grain or what I should check with my chisels to stop this happening?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Consistent splintering at the dovetail pins is almost always caused by dull corners rather than incorrect grain management, so your primary focus should be restoring a true edge to your blades. Since serrated or broken tips often reduce the knife's tipping angle, consider rotating your chisel handle in 15-20 degree increments before each cut to dull the corner and retract the chip until the edge is extremely sharp again. Additionally, ensure your lid and stretch cut are neat with generous knives for the initial cuts and good positioning for the final wrenching stage.",
      "source": "target",
