      "content": "The subtle interference you describe is often caused by excessive chisel pressure or using too much horn on the chisel thickness, both of which can force the blade into the grain and cause gumming. Before attributing this entirely to wood moisture or tool angle, ensure your hook angle on the working face is slightly exaggerated to guide the wood fibers cleanly rather than against them. You may also want to minimize vibration by checking your shavings (the strands left on the steel) for consistency and incrementally reducing your roll pressure during the final moments of the stroke.",
